      title: "Walter Rodney and Underdevelopment",
      body: `## A historian and activist

**Walter Rodney** (1942–1980) was a historian from **Guyana**, who studied at the University of the West Indies and at the School of Oriental and African Studies in London, where he wrote a doctoral thesis on the history of the Upper Guinea coast. He taught at the University of Dar es Salaam in Tanzania, at the height of the African independence era, and at the University of the West Indies in Jamaica, from which he was banned in 1968 by the Jamaican government for his political activities, provoking riots. He returned to Guyana, where he founded a political party, the Working People's Alliance, in opposition to the government of Forbes Burnham. In **1980**, at the age of thirty-eight, he was killed by a bomb hidden in a walkie-talkie. A commission of inquiry in 2016 concluded that the government had been responsible.

## How Europe Underdeveloped Africa

In **1972**, Rodney published his most famous book, ***How Europe Underdeveloped Africa***, written in Dar es Salaam for an African audience. Its argument was bold and simple. Africa, Rodney argued, was not "undeveloped" or "backward" by nature. In the fifteenth century, Africa's level of development was comparable to that of many other parts of the world. Africa was **underdeveloped**—actively impoverished—by its relationship with Europe, first through the slave trade, and then through colonialism. The wealth that Europe took from Africa helped Europe develop, while Africa was drained of its people and resources.

The slave trade, Rodney argued, had several effects. It removed millions of young, productive people, at the age when they would have worked, raised families, and contributed to their societies' growth. It encouraged warfare and raiding, and diverted African rulers' energies from productive activity. It destroyed African industries, such as cloth-making and iron-working, by flooding African markets with European imports. It distorted African economies, orienting them toward the export of people rather than the development of agriculture, crafts, and technology. And it fixed Africa in a subordinate position in the world economy, as a supplier of raw materials—first people, then palm oil, cotton, cocoa, and minerals—to Europe.

## Influence

*How Europe Underdeveloped Africa* became one of the most influential books ever written about Africa. It was read widely by students, activists, and political leaders across Africa, the Caribbean, and among African Americans, and it shaped a generation's understanding of the relationship between Africa and the West. It drew on the "**dependency theory**" developed by Latin American economists and scholars such as **Andre Gunder Frank**, which argued that the poverty of the developing world was a product of its exploitation by the developed world, and on the Marxist tradition. It remains in print and widely read today.

## Criticism

Rodney's argument has also been criticized. Some historians argued that he exaggerated Africa's level of development before the trade, and the trade's impact on African economies. **John Thornton**, as we have seen, argued that African rulers controlled the trade and benefited from it, and that the goods they received were valuable, not "trinkets." **David Eltis** argued that the trade, though horrific in human terms, was a small part of Africa's overall economy, and that Africa's export trade was small relative to its total production. Others pointed out that the European imports did not destroy African industries everywhere; African cloth production, for example, continued to flourish in many regions. And some argued that Rodney's framework left little room for African agency, portraying Africans as passive victims of European exploitation.

Defenders of Rodney replied that his critics underestimated the human and demographic costs of the trade, and that the question was not whether some Africans benefited—clearly some rulers and merchants did—but whether the trade harmed African societies as a whole. They pointed to the research of Manning, Inikori, and Nunn as confirming many of his arguments.

## Inikori

The Nigerian historian **Joseph Inikori** has developed a related argument. He emphasizes the "**opportunity cost**" of the trade: the development that might have occurred if Africa's people had remained in Africa and produced goods for trade, rather than being exported. He argues that the trade prevented the growth of a market economy in Africa, at the very time when the Atlantic economy was driving industrialization in Britain. Africa supplied the labor that produced the commodities that fueled European growth, but received little lasting benefit in return.

## The debate continues

The debate over the slave trade's impact on Africa is not merely academic. It bears on questions of responsibility, reparations, and development policy today. It also bears on how Africans understand their own history. Rodney's great contribution was to insist that Africa's poverty had historical causes, which could be understood and changed—that it was not a natural condition. Whatever one thinks of his specific arguments, that insistence changed the way the world thought about Africa.

> **Pause and consider.** Rodney argued that Africa was not undeveloped but underdeveloped by Europe. What is the difference? How would Thornton respond?`,
